# 4. HASHICORP VAULT INTEGRATION WITH KUBERNETES

## Implementation Overview

In this hands-on implementation, HashiCorp Vault is deployed inside a Kubernetes cluster using Helm.

The Kubernetes authentication method allows applications running inside Kubernetes Pods to authenticate with Vault using their Service Accounts.

Vault Agent Injector automatically adds Vault Agent containers to application Pods and injects the required secrets.

## Implementation Steps

| Step | Activity | Location |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Install Vault | KOPS Server |
| 2 | Initialize Vault | Vault Pod |
| 3 | Unseal Vault | Vault Pod |
| 4 | Store Secret | Vault Pod |
| 5 | Enable Kubernetes Auth | Vault Pod |
| 6 | Configure Kubernetes Auth | Vault Pod |
| 7 | Create Policy | Vault Pod |
| 8 | Create Role | Vault Pod |
| 9 | Create Service Account | KOPS Server |
| 10 | Deploy Application | KOPS Server |
| 11 | Verify Secret Injection | KOPS Server |

## Step 1: Install Vault in Kubernetes

Deploy Vault into the Kubernetes cluster using Helm.

Why?

Instead of storing secrets directly inside Kubernetes application configurations, Vault acts as a centralized source for sensitive information.

Create a namespace:

```bash
kubectl create namespace vault
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get ns
```

## Step 2: Install Helm

Run on the KOPS Server:

```bash
curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3

chmod 700 get_helm.sh

./get_helm.sh

helm version
```

## Step 3: Add HashiCorp Helm Repository

```bash
helm repo add hashicorp https://helm.releases.hashicorp.com
```

Update the repository:

```bash
helm repo update
```

## Step 4: Install Vault

```bash
helm install vault hashicorp/vault -n vault
```

Verify the Pods:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n vault
```

Expected components include:

```text
vault-0
vault-agent-injector-...
```

## Step 5: Enter Vault Pod

Run on the KOPS Server:

```bash
kubectl exec -it vault-0 -n vault -- sh
```

## Step 6: Check Vault Status

Run inside the Vault Pod:

```bash
vault status
```

Expected initial status:

```text
Initialized: false
Sealed: true
```

### What is Sealed and Unsealed?

Vault starts in a sealed state.

When Vault is sealed, it cannot access the encryption keys required to read stored secrets.

During initialization, Vault generates key shares.

In the Shamir seal configuration used in this practice, the default is five key shares, with three required to unseal Vault.

## Step 7: Initialize Vault

Run inside the Vault Pod:

```bash
vault operator init
```

Vault generates:

- Unseal Keys.
- Initial Root Token.

**Important:** Save these credentials securely. Never upload real Unseal Keys or Root Tokens to GitHub.

## Step 8: Unseal Vault

Use the required number of different unseal keys:

```bash
vault operator unseal
```

Enter an unseal key when prompted.

Repeat with the required key shares.

Check Vault status:

```bash
vault status
```

Expected:

```text
Sealed: false
```

## Step 9: Login to Vault

Run inside the Vault Pod:

```bash
vault login
```

Enter the initial Root Token securely when prompted.

Why?

Administrative access is required to configure authentication methods, policies, and secrets.

## Step 10: Enable KV Secret Engine

Enable the KV Version 2 secret engine:

```bash
vault secrets enable -path=secret kv-v2
```

Verify:

```bash
vault secrets list
```

## Step 11: Store Application Secret

Store the database credentials:

```bash
vault kv put secret/mysql username=root password=<YOUR_DB_PASSWORD>
```

Retrieve the secret:

```bash
vault kv get secret/mysql
```

Why?

Applications should not hardcode database credentials. Vault provides a centralized and controlled location for managing them.

## Step 12: Enable Kubernetes Authentication

Run inside the Vault Pod:

```bash
vault auth enable kubernetes
```

Verify:

```bash
vault auth list
```

## Step 13: Get Kubernetes API Server Details

Open a second terminal on the KOPS Server.

Run:

```bash
kubectl config view \
--minify \
-o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}'
```

This command returns the Kubernetes API Server URL.

## Step 14: Get Kubernetes CA Certificate

Run on the KOPS Server:

```bash
kubectl config view --raw \
--minify \
--flatten \
-o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.certificate-authority-data}' \
| base64 -d > ca.crt
```

## Step 15: Copy CA Certificate into Vault Pod

```bash
kubectl cp ca.crt vault/vault-0:/tmp/ca.crt
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl exec -it vault-0 -n vault -- ls /tmp
```

## Step 16: Create Vault Authentication Service Account

Run on the KOPS Server:

Create a YAML file:

```bash
vi vault-auth.yaml
```

Add the following configuration:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: vault-auth
  namespace: vault
```

Apply the configuration:

```bash
kubectl apply -f vault-auth.yaml
```

## Step 17: Generate Service Account Token

```bash
kubectl create token vault-auth -n vault
```

Save the generated token securely for the next configuration step.

## Step 18: Configure Kubernetes Authentication

Enter the Vault Pod terminal.

Run:

```bash
vault write auth/kubernetes/config \
token_reviewer_jwt="<SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN>" \
kubernetes_host="<KUBERNETES_API_SERVER_URL>" \
kubernetes_ca_cert=@/tmp/ca.crt
```

Replace the placeholders with the actual values from your cluster.

Expected output:

```text
Success! Data written
```

## Step 19: Create Vault Policy

Create the policy file:

```bash
cd /tmp

vi mysql-policy.hcl
```

Add:

```hcl
path "secret/data/mysql" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
```

Save the file.

Write the policy to Vault:

```bash
vault policy write mysql-policy mysql-policy.hcl
```

This policy allows authorized applications to read the MySQL secret.

## Step 20: Create Application Service Account

Run on the KOPS Server.

Create a file:

```bash
vi mysql-reader.yaml
```

Add:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: mysql-reader
  namespace: default
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f mysql-reader.yaml
```

## Step 21: Create Vault Role

Run inside the Vault Pod:

```bash
vault write auth/kubernetes/role/mysql-role \
bound_service_account_names=mysql-reader \
bound_service_account_namespaces=default \
policies=mysql-policy \
ttl=24h
```

This connects the Kubernetes Service Account with the Vault policy.

The authentication relationship is:

```text
Application
     |
     v
Service Account
     |
     v
Vault Role
     |
     v
Vault Policy
     |
     v
Secret Access
```

## Step 22: Enable Vault Agent Injector

Run on the KOPS Server:

```bash
helm upgrade vault hashicorp/vault \
-n vault \
--set injector.enabled=true
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n vault
```

Expected components:

```text
vault-0
vault-agent-injector
```

The injector automatically adds Vault Agent containers to application Pods.

## Step 23: Create RoleBinding

Run on the KOPS Server:

```bash
kubectl create clusterrolebinding vault-auth-binding \
--clusterrole=system:auth-delegator \
--serviceaccount=vault:vault-auth
```

This grants the Vault authentication Service Account the permissions required for Kubernetes token review.

## Step 24: Deploy Application with Vault Annotations

Create the application YAML file:

```bash
vi app.yaml
```

Add:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx
  annotations:
    vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject: "true"
    vault.hashicorp.com/role: "mysql-role"
    vault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject-secret-db.txt: "secret/data/mysql"

spec:
  serviceAccountName: mysql-reader

  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
```

Apply the configuration:

```bash
kubectl apply -f app.yaml
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

## Step 25: Verify Secret Injection

Enter the Nginx Pod:

```bash
kubectl exec -it nginx -- sh
```

Move to the Vault secrets directory:

```bash
cd /vault/secrets
```

Check the injected secret:

```bash
cat db.txt
```

The database credentials should be available in the injected secret file.

This demonstrates how an application can access secrets without hardcoding passwords inside the application configuration.

## Step 26: Troubleshooting

Describe the Pod:

```bash
kubectl describe pod nginx
```

Check containers inside the Pod:

```bash
kubectl get pod nginx -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'
```

Check Vault Agent Init logs:

```bash
kubectl logs nginx -c vault-agent-init
```

Check Vault Agent logs:

```bash
kubectl logs nginx -c vault-agent
```

Recreate the application Pod if necessary:

```bash
kubectl delete -f app.yaml

kubectl apply -f app.yaml
```

