# 5. VAULT SECRET INJECTION WORKFLOW

## How Vault Agent Injector Works

When Kubernetes creates an application Pod, the Vault Admission Webhook intercepts the Pod creation request.

It adds Vault Agent containers to the Pod.

### Original Pod

```text
nginx
```

### Modified Pod

```text
nginx
vault-agent-init
vault-agent
```

### Complete Secret Injection Flow

1. Developer stores the secret in Vault.
2. Kubernetes creates the application Pod.
3. Vault Agent Injector intercepts Pod creation.
4. Vault Agent containers are injected.
5. Vault Agent uses the Kubernetes Service Account token.
6. Vault authenticates the application.
7. Kubernetes API Server validates the Service Account token.
8. Vault checks the associated Role and Policy.
9. Vault retrieves the requested secret.
10. Vault Agent writes the secret into the shared volume.
11. The application reads the secret file.

## Vault Authentication Flow Diagram

```text
Application Pod
      |
      | JWT Token
      v
Vault Agent
      |
      v
Vault Kubernetes Auth
      |
      | Validate Token
      v
Kubernetes API Server
      |
      | Valid
      v
Vault Role Check
      |
      v
Vault Policy Check
      |
      v
Read Secret
      |
      v
Inject Secret
      |
      v
Application
```

## Vault Secret Storage Architecture

```text
                  Kubernetes API
                        |
                        v
              Validate Service Account
                        |
       +----------------------------------+
       |        Kubernetes Cluster        |
       |                                  |
       |        +------------------+      |
       |        |    Nginx Pod     |      |
       |        |                  |      |
       |        |   Nginx          |      |
       |        |                  |      |
       |        |   Vault Agent    |      |
       |        |                  |      |
       |        | /vault/secrets/  |      |
       |        |     db.txt       |      |
       |        +------------------+      |
       |                                  |
       +----------------+-----------------+
                        |
                        v
              +--------------------+
              |  HashiCorp Vault   |
              |                    |
              |   secret/mysql     |
              |                    |
              |  Policies & Roles  |
              +--------------------+
```

## What is JWT (JSON Web Token)?

JWT stands for JSON Web Token.

It is a standard format for transmitting identity information between systems.

Think of JWT as a digital ID card that helps prove who you are.

In this implementation, the Kubernetes Service Account token is used by Vault Agent to authenticate the application with Vault.

